 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://moderngov.lambeth.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-23","committee":"Audit and Risk Committee","time":"5.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://moderngov.lambeth.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-29","committee":"Pensions Committee","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://moderngov.lambeth.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-07","committee":"Children's Services Scrutiny Sub-Committee","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed; Moved from 01/07","webcast":false,"url":"https://moderngov.lambeth.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-21","committee":"Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://moderngov.lambeth.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Lambeth still forecasts \u00a39.3m overspend despite \u00a3116m emergency government bailout","lead":"Lambeth Cabinet heard on 23 September that despite receiving \u00a3116m exceptional financial support (emergency government borrowing to cover day-to-day spending that cannot be balanced from council finances) in February, the council is forecasting a \u00a39.303m General Fund overspend for 2026-27. The council's single largest income failure is parking revenue, tracking at only \u00a30.5m against a \u00a36.7m target due to paused schemes and reduced compliance. Growth and Environment is forecasting an \u00a38.6m overspend driven by \u00a310m income shortfalls across parks events, markets, CCTV and sports facilities. The council remains under increased scrutiny from the Ministry of Housing, Communities and Local Government, including an independent review by accountancy firm CIPFA, with explicit government expectation that assets should be disposed of to repay the emergency support.","iso":"2026-09-23","paper_title":"Quarter 1 Budget Monitor 2026-27","paper_url":"https://moderngov.lambeth.gov.uk/documents/s179274/179274.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-23","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"June 26 (Q1) Financial Performance Report 2026-27","paper_url":"https://moderngov.lambeth.gov.uk/documents/s179275/179275.pdf","headline":"Housing Revenue Account forecast \u00a32.5m surplus from reduced borrowing costs and void losses; HRA more stable than General Fund","detail":"The HRA is forecast to achieve a \u00a32.544m surplus in 2026-27, driven by lower PWLB interest rates on refinanced debt and reduced void losses. This represents the HRA is operationally performing better than the General Fund and managing its tenant base effectively.","category":"other","matters":"Provides offsetting positive news; shows HRA management is functioning; however, improvement is due to external interest rate environment rather than operational change, so may not persist.","figures":"\u00a32.544m HRA surplus forecast; includes \u00a318m of \u00a327m disrepair provision","quote":"The HRA is currently forecast to achieve \u00a32.544m surplus for 2026/27. The forecast includes \u00a318m of the \u00a327m provision to account for the ongoing financial pressures associated with resolving the historic housing disrepair claims.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-23","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"June 26 (Q1) Financial Performance Report 2026-27","paper_url":"https://moderngov.lambeth.gov.uk/documents/s179275/179275.pdf","headline":"Council Tax collection 0.8% below profile at Q1 with \u00a31.8m billing delays; resident affordability pressures evident","detail":"Council Tax collection at end Q1 was \u00a3581k below profile. The council notes 'broader economic and affordability pressures affecting taxpayers' ability to maintain payments' with increased direct debit failures and cancelled payment arrangements. Valuation Office delays in banding 875 new properties has also caused an estimated \u00a31.8m in billing delays.","category":"income","matters":"Council Tax is the council's most reliable and stable funding source. Collection below profile combined with stated affordability pressures suggests this will persist; \u00a31.8m billing delay means income recognition is also delayed.","figures":"\u00a3581k shortfall at Q1; \u00a31.8m estimated unbilled from property banding delays","quote":"This all tends to indicate a broader economic and affordability pressures affecting taxpayers' ability to maintain payments. In addition, the Valuation Office delays in banding new Council Tax properties (875) is delaying billing, this is estimated at \u00a31.8m unbilled based on band D.","material":false},{"iso":"2026-09-23","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"June 26 (Q1) Financial Performance Report 2026-27","paper_url":"https://moderngov.lambeth.gov.uk/documents/s179275/179275.pdf","headline":"General Fund earmarked reserves remain low despite EFS top-up; stated to offer little financial resilience","detail":"Although the General Fund Balance is now at the S151 officer's recommended minimum (10% of net revenue expenditure) following EFS, earmarked revenue reserve levels are explicitly described as 'low and offer little financial resilience in the context of the challenges ahead.' This leaves the council with minimal buffer for unexpected costs or demand spikes.","category":"reserves","matters":"Low reserves mean the council cannot absorb unexpected pressures or revenue shortfalls. The council is one significant adverse event away from requiring further government support.","figures":"General Fund Balance at ~10% of expenditure (minimum recommended); earmarked reserves characterised as 'low'","quote":"Although, following the approval of EFS, General Fund Balance is now around the level recommended by the S151 officer (10% of net revenue expenditure), earmarked revenue reserve levels remain low and offer little financial resilience in the context of the challenges ahead.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-23","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"June 26 (Q1) Financial Performance Report 2026-27","paper_url":"https://moderngov.lambeth.gov.uk/documents/s179275/179275.pdf","headline":"Temporary Accommodation savings of \u00a35.5m not being achieved in 2026-27; implementation delays pushing full impact to 2027-28","detail":"Housing Services (General Fund) is forecasting a \u00a35.6m overspend, driven by Temporary Accommodation. Planned savings through long leases (\u00a31.5m), nightly paid reductions (\u00a34.8m before offsets), and incentive schemes (\u00a33m) are not being implemented on schedule. The council now expects full year savings only in 2027-28.","category":"savings","matters":"Temporary Accommodation is the council's highest-cost service and primary savings target. Implementation delays indicate the transformation programme cannot execute its core financial plans on time.","figures":"\u00a35.576m Housing Services overspend; \u00a31.5m long lease, \u00a31.5m transfer, \u00a31m housing company savings all delayed","quote":"Due to the delay to date in their implementation, the total 2026/27 saving is unlikely to be fully achieved, however it is expected that the full year impact will be delivered in 2027-28.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-23","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"June 26 (Q1) Financial Performance Report 2026-27","paper_url":"https://moderngov.lambeth.gov.uk/documents/s179275/179275.pdf","headline":"Dedicated Schools Grant cumulative deficit reaching \u00a311.2m by March 2027; High Needs Block overspend driven by EHCP commitments","detail":"The DSG is forecast to end 2026-27 with a \u00a311.225m cumulative deficit due to High Needs Block overspends. Increases in EHCP commitments and associated top-up costs continue to exceed funding. The council remains reliant on DSG statutory override arrangements, which hold deficits separately but are not a sustainable solution.","category":"demand","matters":"SEND pressures are not being managed within budget, indicating demand-led costs are growing faster than funding. This is a recurring structural deficit in an area of rising national demand.","figures":"\u00a311.225m net DSG deficit forecast at 31 March 2027; \u00a313.4m in-year overspend","quote":"This would leave a net DSG deficit position at 31st March 2027 of \u00a311.225m.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-23"},"name":"Lambeth","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-23","since_iso":"2026-08-24","distress":true,"headline":"Housing Revenue Account forecast \u00a32.5m surplus from reduced borrowing costs and void losses; HRA more stable than General Fund","lead":"Provides offsetting positive news; shows HRA management is functioning; however, improvement is due to external interest rate environment rather than operational change, so may not persist.","also":["15 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Cabinet on 2026-09-23","n_findings":16,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"June 26 (Q1) Financial Performance Report 2026-27","top_paper_url":"https://moderngov.lambeth.gov.uk/documents/s179275/179275.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":122,"news_drafts_total":1}}